      ],
      content: `# Common Python Bugs

Learn to recognize these common bugs - they'll save you hours of debugging!

## 1. Off-By-One Errors

\`\`\`python
# Wrong: goes to 9, not 10
for i in range(10):  # 0-9, not 1-10

# Wrong: index out of range
lst = [1, 2, 3]
lst[3]  # Error! Valid indices are 0, 1, 2

# Wrong: fence post error
# "5 fence posts, how many gaps?" → 4, not 5
\`\`\`

## 2. Mutation Bugs

\`\`\`python
# Aliasing problem
a = [1, 2, 3]
b = a  # b is same list!
b.append(4)
print(a)  # [1, 2, 3, 4] - surprise!

# Fix: make a copy
b = a[:]
\`\`\`

## 3. == vs =

\`\`\`python
x = 5     # Assignment
x == 5    # Comparison (True/False)

# Common bug:
if x = 5:  # SyntaxError! Use ==
\`\`\`

## 4. None Returns

\`\`\`python
def add_to_list(item, lst):
    lst.append(item)  # Returns None!
    
result = add_to_list(4, [1, 2, 3])
print(result)  # None, not [1, 2, 3, 4]
\`\`\`

## 5. Integer Division

\`\`\`python
5 / 2   # 2.5 (true division)
5 // 2  # 2 (integer division)

# Bug when you wanted integer:
average = sum(nums) / len(nums)  # Might be float!
\`\`\`

## 6. Scope Issues

\`\`\`python
x = 10

def change_x():
    x = 20  # Creates local x, doesn't change global!

change_x()
print(x)  # Still 10
\`\`\`

## 7. Mutable Default Arguments

\`\`\`python
# DANGEROUS!
def append_to(item, lst=[]):
    lst.append(item)
    return lst

print(append_to(1))  # [1]
print(append_to(2))  # [1, 2] - not [2]!
\`\`\``,
